 ],
 "literal": "Upon that which rejoiced, like drinking toddy, / upon that which was desired, you experienced hotly, / the salt-carrying cart that stood on the difficult slope, / like dissolving when heavy rain poured down, she, / seeing the natural beauty of her dark, abundant hair.",
 "translation": "As if drinking toddy on top of already being happy, you have fallen passionately in love on top of your desire, upon seeing the natural beauty of her dark, abundant hair—she who is like a salt-carrying cart standing on a difficult slope, dissolving when a heavy rain pours down.",
 "modern": "ஏற்கனவே மகிழ்ச்சியாக இருந்தபோது கள் குடித்தது போலவும், ஏற்கனவே ஆசைப்பட்டதன் மேல் மேலும் தீவிரமாக ஆசைப்பட்டது போலவும் நீ அவளைக் கண்டு உருகுகிறாய். அரிய சரிவில் நின்ற உப்பு ஏற்றிய வண்டி, பெருமழை பெய்தவுடன் கரைந்து போவது போல, அவளுடைய கரிய அடர்ந்த கூந்தலின் இயற்கை அழகைக் கண்டு நீ உருகிப் போயிருக்கிறாய்.",
 "explanation": "The hero is speaking to his own heart after seeing the heroine. He describes how his desire has intensified to an overwhelming degree, comparing it to drinking toddy when already happy. He feels completely dissolved and helpless, like a salt cart on a steep slope that melts away when a heavy rain pours down, all because of the natural beauty of her dark, abundant hair. The image of the salt cart dissolving in the rain beautifully conveys the vulnerability and complete loss of self-control of the lover. This belongs to the Kuriñci tiṇai, where steep slopes and heavy rains are characteristic.",
